## 2. Setting & Aesthetic

**Setting:** A fictional US city, mid-1990s. Player's home neighborhood is run-down — boarded windows, weed-cracked sidewalks, neon signs missing letters. Other neighborhoods (when added post-MVP) range from working-class to affluent.

**Why the 90s:**
- No smartphones — pagers, payphones, paper maps create gameplay friction (this is now a load-bearing design choice, see §7)
- Weed is unambiguously illegal everywhere, raises stakes
- Aesthetic is well-trodden and pixel-art-friendly
- Lets the writing engage with the era's specific anxieties
- Slim jims and car radios actually work as period-correct loot vectors (see §5.5)

**Visual direction:**
- Stardew Valley as the structural reference (top-down, similar character proportions)
- Palette pivots hard from Stardew's saturated greens/yellows to greys, browns, sodium-vapor oranges, dirty teals
- **Palette baseline: Waldgeist** (39 colors, Lospec — warm desaturated, tagged murky/dark/nostalgic). Expect to drop 3-5 colors and add 3-5
- Strong use of warm interior light vs. cold exterior light for mood
- Rain and night are visual events, not just lighting toggles

**Asset pipeline:**
- Tilesets split by function (interior_floor, interior_wall, outdoor_ground, outdoor buildings, outdoor_decoration) rather than by aesthetic — same node structure across rooms means swapping a tileset is a one-line change
- Outdoor terrain uses 47-tile autotile templates (purchased asset pack) configured as Godot terrain sets. Probability weighting handles variant tiles within a terrain
- Hand-authored work concentrates on characters, key props, decorations. Terrain/ground is largely purchased + recolored
- Palette discipline upstream: every new tile passes through an indexed-palette pass in Aseprite before entering Godot

